    "title": "Restaurant in South Carolina's seafood capital committed to local shrimp service amid global market turmoil - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Adrianna Lawrence ,\n2 hours ago\nMURRELLS INLET, S.C. (WBTW) — The FDA is continuing to recall frozen shrimp from Indonesia after reports of possible contamination associated with a radioactive element.\nSince August the FDA has issued 10 recalls nationally, four of them affecting South Carolina Kroger and Food Lion chains.\nOne Murrells Inlet restaurant, Lees Inlet Kitchen says not only do they advertise local fresh shrimp, but they serve it too.\nBetween false advertising claims leveled at some Charleston restaurants by local shrimpers and multiple frozen shrimp recalls, you may find yourself asking where can I find local fresh shrimp?\n“When I was about six, seven, eight and I always liked to ride with my dad down to\nMcClellanville and watch how that factory, you know, the boats backing in and how they got all the shrimp off,” Ashton Dubose, the restaurant’s back house manager said.\nMurrells Inlet is known as the seafood capital of South Carolina. Since 1948, Lees Inlet Kitchen has served up fresh shrimp to locals and tourists who walk through their doors.\n“That’s what our great grandparents did, my granddad did and my parents and it’s just been carried down,” she said. “We don’t know it any other way.”\nAshton Dubose and her sister run the longest standing restaurant in Murrells Inlet.\nDubose says they get all their shrimp from McClellanville and Georgia. She says they typically receive about three loads of shrimp per week and during the summer peel and devein 150 pounds per day handled by six people.\nBut with rising costs and overfished areas, she says it’s harder than ever to maintain what they’re known for.\n“Back in the day, it was an easy couple of hours,” she said. “Those guys work so hard to search for it because it’s not readily available like it used to be. This place is growing.”\nBut she says costs won’t lead them to abandon their longstanding values.\n“Our legacy is more important than, you know, trying to make a dollar by going cheaper or buying something that isn’t what we say it is,’ she said. “I hope that there’s not a day where we can’t get what we need. But I think along with that comes honesty. So, if you can’t get it, don’t advertise it.”\nNews13 asked two Murrells Inlet restaurants if they served fresh, local shrimp and they could not immediately give us an answer.\n* * *\nAdrianna Lawrence is a multimedia journalist at News13. Adrianna is originally from Virginia Beach, Virginia, and joined the News13 team in June 2023 after graduating from Virginia Commonwealth University in May 2023. Keep up with Adrianna on Instagram, Facebook , and X, formerly Twitter . You can also read more of her work here .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WBTW.\n1",
